00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:07,840
So it can be confusing. Why is it because no test is perfect?

00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:12,840
Even though things are a gold standard, there really isn't anything.

00:04:12,840 --> 00:04:25,840
Now, visual presentation of the tuber in tests like a CT scan or an MRI may be considered the gold standard solely because

00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:36,840
you can physically see that the size of the tumor is getting larger if you have a solid tumor and you could use a CT or an MRI.

00:04:36,840 --> 00:04:46,840
A PET scan isn't technically a visual field test. It's measuring uptake of glucose and increase in metabolic rate.

00:04:46,840 --> 00:04:59,840
But it's kind of the same line, whereas a blood test like this or a tuber marker test is measuring an outcome of cancer growth or cancer death.

00:04:59,840 --> 00:05:07,840
So again, no test is perfect. You look at one test and you compare it with other objective tests.

00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:23,840
One parent with subjective findings, meaning your symptoms and what else is going on in your body, how you're feeling, other signs like I'm getting fluid in my abdomen or I'm getting a sighties built up.

00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:29,840
And the sighties is getting worse on a weekly basis, meaning they have to drain more each week.

00:05:29,840 --> 00:05:44,840
That would be a sign that the cancer is getting worse or it's getting better. And I'm getting, you know, even though there was there was 1500 milligram milliliters taken out one time now, it's, you know, now we're only taking out 750 milliliters every week or every two weeks.

00:05:44,840 --> 00:05:51,840
So that would be getting better. So you don't measure just one test. You look at as many tests as possible.

